This is the standard binary-trees benchmark. The output format is:

- `stretch tree of depth N+1\t check: count`
- `iterations\t trees of depth d\t check: sum`
- `long lived tree of depth N\t check: count`

The minimum depth is 4 and maxDepth = max(minDepth+2, N). The stretch depth is maxDepth+1. For small N the standard program uses maxDepth = max(6, N), which is what I follow here.

The task statement says "for each depth d in 4, 6, ..., N build 2^(N-d+4) trees", so iterations = 1 << (maxDepth - d + minDepth).

To make it fast, I use a flat typed-array representation instead of node objects. Each tree is built in a preallocated Int32Array pool. Trees are never held for long, so the work reduces to building and counting.

The simplest fast approach is objects with left/right fields, which V8 handles well. I use that, with a `check` function that recurses. A node is `{l, r}`, and a leaf has null children.

Nodes are created with a class, `class Node { l: Node|null; r: Node|null }`, and `bottomUp(d)` returns `new Node(d>0 ? bottomUp(d-1) : null, d>0 ? bottomUp(d-1) : null)`. `check(n)` returns `1 + (n.l ? check(n.l) + check(n.r!) : 0)`.

The output is collected into an array of lines and joined with newlines.

```typescript
class TreeNode {
  l: TreeNode | null;
  r: TreeNode | null;
  constructor(l: TreeNode | null, r: TreeNode | null) {
    this.l = l;
    this.r = r;
  }
}

function bottomUp(d: number): TreeNode {
  if (d > 0) {
    return new TreeNode(bottomUp(d - 1), bottomUp(d - 1));
  }
  return new TreeNode(null, null);
}

function check(n: TreeNode): number {
  if (n.l === null) return 1;
  return 1 + check(n.l) + check(n.r as TreeNode);
}

function main(): void {
  const N = parseInt(process.argv[2], 10);
  const minDepth = 4;
  const maxDepth = Math.max(minDepth + 2, N);
  const stretch = maxDepth + 1;
  const out: string[] = [];

  out.push(`stretch tree of depth ${stretch}\t check: ${check(bottomUp(stretch))}`);

  const longLived = bottomUp(maxDepth);

  for (let d = minDepth; d <= maxDepth; d += 2) {
    const iters = 1 << (maxDepth - d + minDepth);
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < iters; i++) {
      sum += check(bottomUp(d));
    }
    out.push(`${iters}\t trees of depth ${d}\t check: ${sum}`);
  }

  out.push(`long lived tree of depth ${maxDepth}\t check: ${check(longLived)}`);
  console.log(out.join("\n"));
}

main();
```

